v",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":["Get your personalised ","Pregnancy"," plan"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500","children":"This sample plan is generic. MealCoreAI personalises every meal based on your region, cook time, allergies, and food preferences."}]]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://mealcoreai.com/app/sign-up","className":"shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-white font-semibold text-sm shadow-lg transition-colors bg-orange-500 hover:bg-orange-600 shadow-orange-200","children":["Get My Personalised ","Pregnancy"," Plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-4 w-4","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-gray-50","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Why Indian Food Is Ideal for ","Pregnancy"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 mb-10 max-w-2xl","children":["Your kitchen is already stocked with some of the most clinically researched ingredients for ","pregnancy"," management. Here's what the science says about three of them."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-10","children":[["$","div","Ragi (Finger Millet): for Calcium",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Ragi (Finger Millet): for Calcium"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Ragi has more calcium per gram than milk. That's not marketing — it's nutritional fact. 100g of ragi contains around 344mg of calcium compared to 125mg in 100ml of milk. For Indian women who are lactose intolerant or find it hard to drink the volume of milk recommended during pregnancy, ragi is a genuinely practical solution. Ragi dosas, ragi roti, ragi porridge, ragi idli — the ways to eat it are almost endless. Starting a ragi habit in the second trimester is one of the most impactful dietary choices a pregnant Indian woman can make."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.nin.res.in","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Nutritive Value of Indian Foods, National Institute of Nutrition (NIN), ICMR, 2017."}]]}],["$","div","Palak (Spinach): for Folate and Iron",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Palak (Spinach): for Folate and Iron"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Folate from food matters more in the first 28 days of pregnancy than almost any other nutrient — often before a woman even knows she is pregnant. Spinach is India's most accessible, affordable folate source. A regular serving of palak sabzi provides around 60mcg of folate. Paired with tomatoes or amla (for vitamin C which boosts iron absorption), a daily palak habit addresses two of the biggest pregnancy nutrition gaps simultaneously: folate deficiency and iron deficiency anaemia."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1307234/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Czeizel AE, Dudás I, New England Journal of Medicine, 1992; NIN India, 2017."}]]}],["$","div","Dates (Khajoor): for Iron and Labour",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Dates (Khajoor): for Iron and Labour"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Dates are a traditional pregnancy food in Indian and Middle Eastern cultures, and there's now clinical research supporting the practice. A study found that women who consumed 6 dates daily in the last 4 weeks of pregnancy had significantly shorter first stage of labour and higher cervical dilation on admission. Beyond labour benefits, dates are one of the most iron-dense sweet foods available — two or three dates a day provide meaningful iron alongside natural sugars that won't spike blood sugar the way refined sweets do."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21280989/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Al-Kuran O et al., Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology, 2011."}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Regional ","Pregnancy"," Meal Plan Variations"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl","children":["Managing ","pregnancy"," through food looks different depending on where in India you cook. Here's how it adapts across three major food traditions."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🌴 South Indian"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["South Indian cooking is built around rice, lentils, and fermented foods, all of which can be adapted for ","pregnancy"," management. Swap white rice for ragi mudde or foxtail millet pongal, keep your sambar and rasam (they're excellent), and lean on pesarattu and dosas for high-protein breakfasts."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/pregnancy-weekly-meal-plan-south-indian","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See South Indian ","Pregnancy"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🌾 North Indian"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["The roti-dal-sabzi structure of North Indian cooking is one of the most naturally adaptable frameworks for ","pregnancy",". Switch wheat atta to bajra or jowar flour, choose mustard oil or olive oil over vanaspati, and keep portions of dal generous. It's your best protein and fibre source."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/pregnancy-weekly-meal-plan-north-indian","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See North Indian ","Pregnancy"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🎪 Gujarati"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["Gujarati food traditions (dhokla, khichdi, thepla, handvo) are naturally portion-controlled and often dal-forward. For ","pregnancy",", the traditional Gujarati thali works well with small adjustments: less jaggery in sabzis, whole grain thepla instead of maida rotla, and moong dal khichdi as a staple dinner."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/pregnancy-weekly-meal-plan-gujarati","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See Gujarati ","Pregnancy"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-10 bg-gray-50","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-6 flex gap-4","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-2xl shrink-0","children":"⚕️"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2","children":"When to See a Doctor"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":["Diet is one of the most powerful tools for managing ","pregnancy",", but it works best alongside proper medical care. If you're newly diagnosed, experiencing severe symptoms, considering stopping medication, or your symptoms are worsening despite dietary changes, please consult your doctor or a specialist. MealCoreAI's meal plans are designed to complement medical treatment, not replace it. The nutrition guidance on this page is for educational purposes and does not constitute medical advice."]}]]}]]}]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Pregnancy"," Diet: Frequently Asked Questions"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-500 mb-10","children":["Evidence-based answers to the most common questions about ","Pregnancy"," nutrition."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","details","What should an Indian pregnant woman eat every day?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"What should an Indian pregnant woman eat every day?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"An Indian pregnant woman's daily diet should include: at least one serving of dal or legumes for folate and protein; one green leafy vegetable (palak, methi, or moringa) for iron and folate; two servings of dairy for calcium; a seasonal fruit for vitamins; and a source of healthy fat like ghee, walnuts, or coconut. Ragi should replace some wheat rotis for its superior calcium content. Sesame seeds (til) sprinkled on food provide extraordinary calcium. Eggs (if non-vegetarian) are ideal — they provide iodine, choline, protein, and DHA in one food. Moringa leaves, dried or fresh, are an underutilised pregnancy superfood rich in iron, calcium, and protein that deserves a daily place in Indian pregnancy diets."}]]}],["$","details","How should my diet change each trimester in India?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"How should my diet change each trimester in India?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"In the first trimester (weeks 1-12), focus on folate (spinach, rajma, dal, lemon), vitamin B12 (dairy, eggs), and managing nausea with small frequent meals. Ginger chai, buttermilk, and bland foods like rice kanji work well. In the second trimester (weeks 13-26), increase iron intake dramatically — include dark leafy vegetables, dates, and ragi daily. Pair iron foods with vitamin C (amla, lemon, guava) for better absorption. Avoid tea and coffee within an hour of meals as they block iron absorption. In the third trimester (weeks 27-40), increase calorie intake by 300-400 kcal, prioritise calcium for fetal bone mineralisation (which accelerates), include DHA-rich foods (fish or walnuts), and eat smaller more frequent meals as the baby presses on the stomach."}]]}],["$","details","Which Indian foods are highest in folate for pregnancy?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Which Indian foods are highest in folate for pregnancy?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Folate is most critical in the first 28 days of pregnancy (often before a woman even knows she is pregnant), making it important for all women of reproductive age to eat folate-rich Indian foods regularly. The best Indian folate sources are: rajma and all kidney beans; chana and Bengal gram; masoor dal; fresh spinach and methi; drumstick leaves (moringa); broccoli and asparagus; and fresh orange and citrus fruits. Many of these are everyday Indian foods, making adequate folate intake achievable through diet. However, most doctors recommend a folic acid supplement (400-800mcg daily) before conception and through the first trimester because dietary folate absorption varies and the stakes are high."}]]}],["$","details","How can an Indian pregnant woman increase haemoglobin naturally?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"How can an Indian pregnant woman increase haemoglobin naturally?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Iron deficiency anaemia affects more than half of Indian pregnant women and is the most common nutritional deficiency in Indian pregnancy. To increase haemoglobin naturally through Indian food: eat palak (spinach) or methi daily in sabzis; include dates and raisins as snacks; eat ragi-based foods regularly (ragi is iron-rich among grains); include drumstick in dal or sabzi weekly; soak rajma and chana overnight before cooking (reduces anti-nutrients that block iron absorption); always pair iron-rich foods with vitamin C sources like lemon, amla, or guava; cook in iron kadhai or pressure cooker (leaches beneficial iron into food); and strictly avoid drinking tea or coffee within one hour of meals. If haemoglobin is below 10, dietary changes alone are usually insufficient and iron supplementation is medically necessary."}]]}],["$","details","Which Indian foods should be completely avoided during pregnancy?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Which Indian foods should be completely avoided during pregnancy?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"The foods most important to avoid during Indian pregnancy are: raw or unripe papaya (contains latex that can stimulate contractions); raw sprouts of any kind (Listeria risk — cook them thoroughly); street food made with raw water or untreated ingredients (infection risk); unpasteurised dairy or unaged soft cheeses; raw or undercooked eggs and meat; high-mercury fish like shark, king mackerel, and swordfish; excessive liver consumption (too much preformed vitamin A); and more than 1-2 cups of chai or coffee daily. Pineapple in large quantities is sometimes advised against in early pregnancy (bromelain content), though moderate consumption of ripe pineapple is generally considered safe. Alcohol has no safe level during pregnancy and should be completely avoided."}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-14 bg-orange-50/60","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-7","children":"More Pregnancy Resources"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4","children":[["$","$L3","/meal-plans/pregnancy-7-day-meal-plan-north-indian",{"href":"/meal-plans/pregnancy-7-day-meal-plan-north-indian","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"North Indian pregnancy 7-day meal plan trimester-wise"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children":["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3.5 w-3.5","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]}]]}],["$","$L3","/blog/pregnancy-nutrition-first-trimester",{"href":"/blog/pregnancy-nutrition-first-trimester","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"First trimester nutrition guide for Indian mothers"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children":["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"c